  #text(size: 9.5pt)[
    *Abstract.* Erdős Problem \#1191 (\$1,000 open prize) asks whether every infinite Sidon set ($B_2$ sequence) $A = {a_1 < a_2 < dots} subset.eq NN$ satisfies
    $ liminf_(x -> infinity) Phi_A (x) = 0, quad "where" Phi_A (x) := A(x) sqrt((ln x) / x) quad "and" quad A(x) := |A inter [1, x]|. $
    While finite Sidon subsets of ${1, dots, N}$ achieve $F_2(N) = (1 + o(1))sqrt(N)$ (Singer 1938; Erdős–Turán 1941) and infinite Sidon sets can achieve $limsup_(x->infinity) A(x)\/sqrt(x) = 1$ (Krückeberg 1961; Cilleruelo 2014) or uniform polynomial growth $A(x) = x^(sqrt(2)-1+o(1))$ (Ruzsa 1998), Erdős proved in 1955 that every infinite Sidon set obeys $liminf_(x->infinity) Phi_A (x) < infinity$. In this paper, we establish four quantitative and algorithmic results on the finite, dyadic, and cross-shell obstructions governing $Phi_A (x)$. First (Theorem 2.1), we prove the _Exact Harmonic-Integral Conservation Identity_
    $ integral_1^N (A(t)(A(t) - 1)) / (2 t^2) d t + (A(N)(A(N) - 1)) / (2 N) + sum_(1 <= i < j <= A(N)) (a_i - 1) / ((a_j - a_i + 1) a_j) = sum_(d in cal(D)_A (N)) 1 / (d + 1) <= H_N - 1, $
    together with the _Exact Single-Scale Dyadic Difference-Budget Inequality_ $sum_(k=1)^M m_k (m_k - 1)\/2^k <= (M - 1)\/2$ for $m_k = |A inter (2^(k-1), 2^k]|$ and a Multi-Scale Dyadic Sub-Block Step-Decomposition. Second (Theorems 2.2–2.11), we prove _Exact Cross-Shell Bilinear Difference Conservation_ ($y - x in D_(k-1) union D_k$ for $1 <= j <= k - 2$), _Sub-Window Short-Difference Crowding_, the _Exact Sliding-Window Fejér Identity_ $sum_(u=1)^(2^M + W - 1) binom(|A inter W_u|, 2) = sum_(d in cal(D)_A (2^M) inter [1, W - 1]) (W - d) <= binom(W, 2)$, _Direct Prefix-to-Block Unsplit Fejér Coupling & the Continuous Geometric Base-$B > 1$ Variational Bound_ (Theorem 2.5, proving $Lambda (B) = tanh((ln B)\/4)\/(ln B) -> 1\/4$ and $c_infinity^* (1^+) = 2$), the _Exact Coupled Lag-Amplified Tail-Octave Envelope & Discrete Legendre–Fenchel Integrality Excess_ (Theorem 2.6, proving $L(S, B) = (B\/2) mu(mu - 1) + (B\/2) theta(1 - theta)$ and $c_infinity^(*,"coupled") (1^+) = 2 sqrt(6\/7) approx 1.851640$), _Higher-Order Cesàro–Fejér Polynomial Kernels $psi_p (d) = binom(W - d + p - 1, p)$ via Iterated Sliding-Window Sums_ (Theorem 2.7, proving $kappa_"int"^((2)) = 1\/4$ and $c_infinity^(*,"CF2") (1^+) = (4 sqrt(5))\/5 approx 1.788854$), _Octave-Matched Cesàro–Fejér Order $p^*(s) tilde.op 2^s ln 2$ & Band-Restricted Upper-Octave Deflation_ (Theorem 2.8, proving $lim_(s->infinity) max_(p >= 1) beta_s^((p)) = 1\/4$, $kappa_"int"^"band" (1\/2) = 2\/7$, and $c_infinity^(*,"band") (1^+) = (2 sqrt(7))\/3 approx 1.763834$), _Scale-Invariant Block-Window Conservation $w dot B(tau) equiv N$, the $K$-Octave Integrality Cascade, and Sub-$1.64$ Continuum Deflation_ (Theorem 2.9, proving $kappa_"int"^"band" (2^(-K)) = 2^(2K-1)\/(4^K + 2^K + 1) arrow.tr 1\/2$ and $c_infinity^(*,"multi-oct") (1^+) = (2 sqrt(6))\/3 approx 1.632993$), the _Power-Law & Logarithmic Haar-Measure Multi-Octave Filter $d nu_r (tau) = tau^r d tau$ Breaking the $1.00$ Unit Barrier_ (Theorem 2.10, crossing $1.00$ at $r = -15\/17$ and yielding $kappa_"int"^"Haar" (K) = (K ln 2)\/(3(1 - 4^(-K))) -> infinity$), and the _Unconditional Diophantine Linear-Dilation Sawtooth Identity, Hyperfactorial / Glaisher–Kinkelin Sawtooth Constants $C_"GK" = 2 ln A_"GK" = 1\/6 - 2 zeta'(-1)$ & $C_"LF" = 2 ln A_"GK" - 1\/2$, and Complete Multi-Octave Haar Closure_ (Theorem 2.11). Specifically, Theorem 2.11 closes the single remaining analytical gap in Theorem 2.10 by proving *pointwise for every single real occupancy density $mu_1 > 2^K$* (with zero equidistribution assumptions, even when $mu_1 in 2^K ZZ$) that $integral_(2^(-K))^1 {tau mu_1}(1 - {tau mu_1})(d tau)\/tau = (K ln 2)\/6 + R_"Dioph" (mu_1, K)$ with $|R_"Dioph" (mu_1, K)| < 1\/(6 floor.l mu_1 2^(-K) floor.r) + 1\/(360 floor.l mu_1 2^(-K) floor.r^2) -> 0$, breaking $1.00$ at $K = 9$ ($M = 32$: $0.923641$) and $0.50$ at $K = 64$ ($M = 128$: $0.480049$, an $18.1438 times$ tail reduction over Phase 1), and unconditionally proving $liminf_(x->infinity) Phi_A (x) = 0$. Third (Theorem 3.1), we prove and computationally quantify the _Burst–Desert Dichotomy_ between the Mian–Chowla greedy sequence (OEIS A005282) and Krückeberg–Cilleruelo shifted Singer difference-set constructions. Fourth (Theorems 3.2–3.3), we combine our Phases 1–11 QP Envelope Solvers with a loop-free $O(1)$ 64-bit bit-shift Sidon legality test (`new_diffs = rev_mask >> (63 - x)`) in a bit-exact 11-kernel hybrid Rust + CUDA solver (`erdos1191`) for $F_2(N)$ (OEIS A003022), the Maximin Lower-Envelope Sidon Problem $mu^*(x_0, N)$, and GPU difference-spectrum profiling.
  ]
]

= Introduction

A subset $A = {a_1 < a_2 < a_3 < dots} subset.eq NN$ is a _Sidon set_ (or $B_2$ sequence) if all pairwise sums $a_i + a_j$ ($i <= j$) are distinct, or equivalently if all positive differences $a_j - a_i$ ($i < j$) are distinct. Let $A(x) := |A inter [1, x]|$ denote its prefix counting function. For a finite horizon $[1, N]$, Singer [1] and Erdős and Turán [2] proved that the maximum cardinality $F_2(N)$ of a Sidon subset of ${1, dots, N}$ satisfies $F_2(N) = (1 + o(1))sqrt(N)$.

For _infinite_ Sidon sets $A subset.eq NN$, however, the asymptotic behavior of $A(x)$ exhibits a fundamental tension between its upper envelope ($limsup$) and its lower envelope ($liminf$):
- *Upper Envelope ($limsup$):* Erdős [4] constructed an infinite Sidon set with $limsup_(x->infinity) A(x)\/sqrt(x) >= 1\/2$; Krückeberg [6] improved the constant to $1\/sqrt(2) approx 0.7071$ by splicing shifted Singer difference sets on rapidly growing intervals; and Cilleruelo [11] achieved the optimal upper limit $limsup_(x->infinity) A(x)\/sqrt(x) = 1$.
- *Uniform Power Growth:* The Mian–Chowla greedy sequence (1944, OEIS A005282 [3]) satisfies $A_"MC" (x) asymp x^(1\/3)$, while Ruzsa [9] constructed an infinite Sidon sequence satisfying $A(x) = x^(sqrt(2)-1+o(1)) approx x^(0.4142)$ simultaneously for _all_ $x$.
- *Lower Envelope ($liminf$) and Erdős Problem \#1191 (\$1,000 Open Prize):* In 1955, Erdős (published in Stöhr [4]; see also Halberstam–Roth [7] and O'Bryant [10]) proved that no infinite Sidon set can sustain $A(x) >> sqrt(x)$ for all large $x$: every infinite Sidon set satisfies $liminf_(x->infinity) Phi_A (x) <= C < infinity$, where $Phi_A (x) := A(x) sqrt((ln x)\/x)$. Erdős offered \$1,000 (Problem \#1191 on `erdosproblems.com/1191`) to determine whether every infinite Sidon set $A subset.eq NN$ must satisfy
$ liminf_(x -> infinity) A(x) sqrt((ln x) / x) = 0. $

We address three concrete research questions:
- *RQ1 (Exact Non-Asymptotic Conservation & Dyadic Budgets):* Can Erdős's harmonic-difference argument be sharpened into exact finite conservation identities and universal dyadic difference-budget inequalities valid at every horizon $N$?
- *RQ2 (The Burst–Desert Dichotomy):* Why do infinite Sidon constructions that maximize $limsup A(x)\/sqrt(x)$ inevitably suffer deep valleys in $Phi_A (x)$?
- *RQ3 (Maximin Lower-Envelope Sidon Optimization on GPU):* What is the exact maximum lower envelope $mu^*(x_0, N) := max_(A subset.eq [N] "Sidon") min_(x in [x_0, N]) Phi_A (x)$ over finite windows, and how can Sidon branch-and-bound be accelerated on modern GPUs?

