= Exact Harmonic-Integral Conservation and Dyadic Difference Budgets

*Proposition 2.0 (Classical Finite Sidon Difference Bound; Singer [1], Erdős–Turán [2]).* _For every integer $N >= 1$ and every Sidon set $A subset.eq {1, dots, N}$, the $binom(|A|, 2)$ positive differences $a_j - a_i$ ($1 <= i < j <= |A|$) are pairwise distinct elements of ${1, dots, N - 1}$, so $binom(|A|, 2) <= N - 1$ and $F_2(N) = (1 + o(1))sqrt(N)$._

Let $A = {a_1 < a_2 < dots} subset.eq NN$ be a Sidon set, and for $N >= 2$, let $m = A(N)$ and $cal(D)_A (N) := {a_j - a_i : 1 <= i < j <= m} subset.eq {1, dots, N - 1}$ denote the positive difference set of $A inter [1, N]$.

*Theorem 2.1 (Exact Harmonic-Integral Conservation and Dyadic Budget Inequalities).* _Let $A subset.eq NN$ be any Sidon set. Then:_
1. _*(Exact Harmonic-Integral Conservation Identity)* For every integer $N >= 2$,_
   $ underbrace(integral_1^N (A(t)(A(t) - 1)) / (2 t^2) d t + (A(N)(A(N) - 1)) / (2 N), cal(E)_A (N) = sum_(j=2)^(A(N)) (j-1)\/a_j) + underbrace(sum_(1 <= i < j <= A(N)) (a_i - 1) / ((a_j - a_i + 1) a_j), Delta_A (N) >= 0) = underbrace(sum_(d in cal(D)_A (N)) 1 / (d + 1), cal(B)_A (N)) <= H_N - 1. $
2. _*(Exact Single-Scale Dyadic Difference-Budget Inequality)* For $k >= 1$, let $I_k := (2^(k-1), 2^k] inter NN$ and $m_k := |A inter I_k|$. For every $M >= 1$,_
   $ sum_(k=1)^M (m_k (m_k - 1)) / 2^k <= 2 sum_(d=1)^(2^(M-1)-1) 2^(-(floor.l log_2 d floor.r + 2)) bold(1)_(d in A - A) <= (M - 1) / 2. $
3. _*(Multi-Scale Dyadic Sub-Block Step-Decomposition)* For every $k >= 1$, depth $S_k >= 0$, and $d in [1, 2^(k-1)]$, the pointwise bound $1\/d >= 2^(-(k-1)) + sum_(s=1)^(S_k) 2^(-(k-s)) bold(1)_(d <= 2^(k-1-s))$ holds, implying_
   $ sum_(k=1)^M ((S_k + 2) m_k^2 - (2^(S_k) + 1) m_k) / 2^(k+1) <= sum_(k=1)^M sum_(x < y in A inter I_k) 1 / (y - x) <= H_(2^(M-1)-1). $

*Proof.* For (1), integrating $binom(A(t), 2)\/t^2 = sum_(1 <= i < j <= m) bold(1)_(a_j <= t)\/t^2$ over $[1, N]$ gives $cal(E)_A (N) = sum_(1 <= i < j <= m) 1\/a_j$. Adding $(a_i - 1)\/((d_(i,j) + 1)a_j) >= 0$ (where $d_(i,j) = a_j - a_i$) yields $1\/(d_(i,j) + 1)$, and the Sidon property ensures that $(i, j) |-> d_(i,j)$ is a bijection onto $cal(D)_A (N) subset.eq {1, dots, N-1}$. For (2), each pair $x < y$ in $I_k$ has $d = y - x <= 2^(k-1) - 1$ (so $k >= floor.l log_2 d floor.r + 2$) and occurs in at most one shell $I_k$ across all of $A$. Summing $2 dot 2^r dot 2^(-(r+2)) = 1\/2$ over $r = floor.l log_2 d floor.r in {0, dots, M-2}$ gives $(M-1)\/2$. Part (3) follows by applying Cauchy–Schwarz to the $2^s$ sub-intervals of $I_k$ of length $2^(k-1-s)$. #h(1fr) *Q.E.D.*

